Below is a quick summary of what’s going on and two small edits you can apply to get Prism to recognize Razor’s `@helper` blocks (and even treat `@helper` as a directive), which in turn will stop everything inside that block from falling back into “plain markup” and being mis-highlighted.

1) **What’s wrong?**  
   The Razor grammar shipped in `prism-cshtml.js` only knows about these top-level directives:

     @addTagHelper, @attribute, @implements, @inherits, @inject, @layout, @model, @namespace,  
     @page, @preservewhitespace, @removeTagHelper, @section, @tagHelperPrefix, @using  

   and only these kinds of code‐blocks:

     @{…}, @code{…}, @for/foreach/lock/switch/using/while (…) {…}, @do {…} while(…);,  
     @try {…} catch(…) {…} finally {…}, @if (…) {…} (else if / else)…  

   It doesn’t know about `@helper … { … }` at all, so once it sees `@helper` it thinks “oh, that’s just HTML” and the entire contents of that block get run back through the HTML rules, everything breaks, the quotes inside your placeholder call blow the HTML tokeniser away, etc.

2) **The fix**  
   We just need to add `helper` to the directive‐regex _and_ insert a new “block” pattern for `@helper NAME(...) { … }`.

